  "steps": [
    {
      "place": "madurai",
      "phase": "vijayanagara",
      "title": "The conquest of Madurai",
      "native": "मधुराविजयम्",
      "lang": "sa",
      "when": "about 1371 to 1378",
      "at": 1,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          78.9,
          11.4
        ],
        "width": 9
      },
      "legs": [
        {
          "from": "kanchipuram",
          "to": "madurai",
          "traveller": "kampana-army",
          "style": "vijayanagara",
          "bend": 0.1
        }
      ],
      "text": "The Later Pandyas had lost Madurai to Delhi’s armies, and from 1335 a sultanate ruled the old Pandya country. Kumara Kampana, a son of Vijayanagara’s king Bukka, took Kanchipuram and then Madurai. His wife Gangadevi told the campaign in a Sanskrit poem, Madhura Vijayam, ‘the conquest of Madurai’, in which a goddess asks him to free the city.",
      "today": "Madurai, Tamil Nadu."
    },
    {
      "place": "madurai",
      "phase": "vijayanagara",
      "title": "Nagama and Viswanatha",
      "native": "விஸ்வநாத நாயக்கர்",
      "when": "about 1529",
      "at": 12,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          77.6,
          12.6
        ],
        "width": 10
      },
      "legs": [
        {
          "from": "hampi",
          "to": "madurai",
          "traveller": "viswanatha",
          "style": "vijayanagara",
          "bend": 0.1
        }
      ],
      "text": "Tradition tells that Krishnadevaraya sent his general Nagama Nayaka to Madurai to restore its Pandya ruler, but Nagama kept the country for himself. His son Viswanatha offered to bring him back, defeated and captured his own father, and was given Madurai to govern as a reward. Viswanatha’s rule, from about 1529, began two centuries of Nayakas.",
      "today": "Madurai’s old city, laid out in squares around the Meenakshi temple."
    },
    {
      "place": "madurai",
      "phase": "vijayanagara",
      "title": "Seventy-two bastions",
      "native": "பாளையக்காரர்",
      "when": "1529 to 1564",
      "at": 17.2,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          78.2,
          10.2
        ],
        "width": 9
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "With his minister Ariyanatha Mudaliar, Viswanatha cleared forest, rebuilt Madurai’s fort and divided the country into military estates (palayams). Their chiefs, the palayakkarars or poligars, collected revenue, kept troops and, by tradition, each guarded one of the fort’s 72 bastions. The system held the south together for two centuries, and later made the poligars hard to rule.",
      "today": "Madurai’s fort walls were pulled down in the 19th century; the Veli streets follow their line."
    },
    {
      "place": "rakkasagi",
      "phase": "kings",
      "title": "News from Talikota",
      "native": "ತಾಳಿಕೋಟೆ",
      "lang": "kn",
      "when": "1565",
      "at": 23.6,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          77.3,
          14.6
        ],
        "width": 11
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "Far to the north, the Deccan Sultanates destroyed Vijayanagara’s army at Talikota in 1565 and sacked its capital. The Vijayanagara Empire lived on further south under the Aravidu kings, but its hold on the Tamil country slackened. The nayakas of Madurai, Thanjavur and Gingee went on honouring the emperor, and paying tribute when it suited them, while ruling as kings.",
      "today": "Talikota, Vijayapura district, Karnataka; the battlefield is thought to lie to its south, by the Krishna."
    },
    {
      "place": "madurai",
      "phase": "kings",
      "title": "A Jesuit in saffron",
      "native": "தத்துவ போதக சுவாமி",
      "when": "1606 to 1645",
      "at": 37,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          78.2,
          10.3
        ],
        "width": 9
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "The Italian Jesuit Roberto de Nobili reached Madurai in 1606. He lived as an Indian ascetic (sannyasi), wore saffron, kept a vegetarian diet, learnt Tamil and Sanskrit and called himself a teacher of truth (Tattuva Bodhakar). His methods divided the Church until Rome allowed them in 1623. Jesuit letters from his mission are a main source for Nayaka history.",
      "today": "De Nobili died at Mylapore, in today’s Chennai, in 1656."
    },
    {
      "place": "tiruchirappalli",
      "phase": "kings",
      "title": "The Rock Fort and Toppur",
      "native": "திருச்சிராப்பள்ளி",
      "when": "1616 to 1634",
      "at": 40.5,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          78.6,
          10.9
        ],
        "width": 9
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "Muttu Virappa Nayaka moved the capital north to Tiruchirappalli, whose fort crowns a rock above the Kaveri. In a war over Vijayanagara’s throne, Madurai and Gingee backed the losing side: at Toppur, near the Kaveri, in about 1616, the Thanjavur Nayaka and the emperor’s general Yachama defeated them, in one of the largest battles of the time in the south.",
      "today": "The Rock Fort and its Ucchi Pillaiyar temple, Tiruchirappalli, Tamil Nadu."
    },
    {
      "place": "madurai",
      "phase": "kings",
      "title": "Tirumala Nayaka’s palace",
      "native": "திருமலை நாயக்கர் மஹால்",
      "when": "1623 to 1659",
      "at": 46.6,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          78.2,
          10.3
        ],
        "width": 9
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "Tirumala Nayaka brought the capital back to Madurai and became its best-known ruler. His palace, built about 1636, joined Dravidian and Islamic styles in huge domes and arches. He dug the Teppakulam, a great temple tank, and by tradition joined the festivals of Meenakshi’s wedding and of the god Alagar into the Chithirai festival held each spring.",
      "today": "Thirumalai Nayakkar Mahal, Madurai: about a quarter of the palace survives."
    },
    {
      "place": "madurai",
      "phase": "kings",
      "title": "The war of noses",
      "native": "மூக்கறுப்புப் போர்",
      "when": "about 1656 to 1659",
      "at": 53,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          77.4,
          11.2
        ],
        "width": 10
      },
      "legs": [
        {
          "from": "srirangapatna",
          "to": "madurai",
          "traveller": "mysore-army",
          "style": "mysore",
          "bend": 0.1
        }
      ],
      "text": "Around 1656, as Tirumala grew old, Mysore’s army invaded and, by Jesuit and Mysore accounts, cut off the noses of captives and sent them home as trophies. Madurai’s army, helped by Raghunatha Sethupathi of Ramnad, drove the invaders out and, by the same accounts, answered in kind inside Mysore. Its Tamil name, ‘the nose-cutting war’, keeps its memory.",
      "today": "The Ramalinga Vilasam palace of the Sethupathis, Ramanathapuram, has murals of their wars."
    },
    {
      "place": "thanjavur",
      "phase": "decline",
      "title": "Thanjavur changes hands",
      "native": "தஞ்சாவூர்",
      "when": "1673 to 1676",
      "at": 59.2,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          78.4,
          11.7
        ],
        "width": 9.5
      },
      "legs": [
        {
          "from": "bengaluru",
          "to": "thanjavur",
          "traveller": "ekoji",
          "style": "maratha",
          "bend": 0.1
        }
      ],
      "text": "Chokkanatha Nayaka of Madurai quarrelled with Vijayaraghava, Nayaka of Thanjavur, and in 1673 took the city; Vijayaraghava was killed and his line ended. A Bijapur general from Bangalore, Ekoji (Venkoji), Shivaji’s half-brother, came to restore it and in 1676 took Thanjavur for himself. His Maratha dynasty ruled there until 1855: Madurai had a dangerous new neighbour.",
      "today": "The Thanjavur Maratha palace and its Saraswathi Mahal library, Thanjavur."
    },
    {
      "place": "madurai",
      "phase": "decline",
      "title": "Rani Mangammal",
      "native": "ராணி மங்கம்மாள்",
      "when": "1689 to about 1704",
      "at": 66,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          78.2,
          10.3
        ],
        "width": 9
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "Mangammal, widow of Chokkanatha, ruled as regent for her infant grandson. She sent gifts to Aurangzeb’s generals to keep the Mughals away, and fought Travancore and Thanjavur when she had to. She built roads, wells and rest houses (chattrams) still named after her. Her palace at Madurai, the Tamukkam, now houses a museum to Mahatma Gandhi.",
      "today": "The Gandhi Memorial Museum in the Tamukkam palace, Madurai."
    },
    {
      "place": "tiruchirappalli",
      "phase": "decline",
      "title": "The last queen",
      "native": "அரசி மீனாட்சி",
      "when": "1731 to 1736",
      "at": 77.5,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          78.9,
          11.9
        ],
        "width": 9
      },
      "legs": [
        {
          "from": "arcot",
          "to": "tiruchirappalli",
          "traveller": "chanda-sahib",
          "style": "arcot",
          "bend": 0.1
        }
      ],
      "text": "Meenakshi, widow of Vijayaranga Chokkanatha, ruled from Tiruchirappalli from 1731 amid rival claims to the throne. Chanda Sahib, a kinsman of the Nawab of Arcot, came south and won her trust; tradition says he swore loyalty on a Quran that was really a wrapped brick. He seized the fort in 1736, and she is said to have taken poison.",
      "today": "Tiruchirappalli; the fight over the Nawab’s throne soon drew French and British armies here."
    },
    {
      "place": "panchalankurichi",
      "phase": "after",
      "title": "The poligars fight on",
      "native": "வீரபாண்டிய கட்டபொம்மன்",
      "when": "1750s to 1801",
      "at": 90,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          78.2,
          9.6
        ],
        "width": 9
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "The palayakkarars outlived the Nayakas. When the Nawab of Arcot and the East India Company demanded their revenue, many resisted: Puli Thevar in the 1750s and 1760s, Veerapandiya Kattabomman of Panchalankurichi, hanged by the Company in 1799, and the Marudu brothers of Sivaganga, executed in 1801. Tamil ballads and a 1959 film keep their story alive.",
      "today": "A memorial fort built by the Tamil Nadu government stands at Panchalankurichi, Thoothukudi district."
    },
    {
      "place": "madurai",
      "phase": "after",
      "title": "The towers of Meenakshi",
      "native": "மீனாட்சி அம்மன் கோயில்",
      "when": "16th and 17th centuries, and today",
      "at": 99,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          78.2,
          10.1
        ],
        "width": 9
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "The Meenakshi temple, damaged in the 14th century, was rebuilt and enlarged under the Nayakas into a city within the city. Its fourteen gateway towers (gopurams) are covered in painted figures; the tallest, on the south, rises about 52 metres. The Hall of a Thousand Pillars, built in 1569 by Ariyanatha Mudaliar, has 985 carved pillars.",
      "today": "Meenakshi Amman temple, Madurai, one of the most visited temples in India."
    }
  ],
