  "people": {
    "heading": "People in this chapter",
    "items": [
      {
        "id": "viswanatha",
        "name": "Viswanatha Nayaka",
        "native": "விஸ்வநாத நாயக்கர்",
        "life": "died about 1563",
        "role": "First Nayaka of Madurai",
        "steps": [
          1,
          2
        ],
        "text": "Son of the general Nagama Nayaka, Viswanatha served at the Vijayanagara court before he was sent to Madurai. Tradition remembers him for choosing loyalty to the emperor over his own father. As governor he rebuilt Madurai’s fort and temples, cleared land for farming and set up the palayam system that his heirs, the Nayakas, kept for two centuries."
      },
      {
        "id": "ariyanatha",
        "name": "Ariyanatha Mudaliar",
        "native": "அரியநாத முதலியார்",
        "life": "16th century",
        "role": "Minister and general (dalavay)",
        "steps": [
          2,
          12
        ],
        "text": "Ariyanatha Mudaliar, from a Vellala family near Kanchipuram, served Viswanatha and the next Nayakas as minister and commander of the army (dalavay). Tradition credits him with planning the palayam system. He built the Hall of a Thousand Pillars in the Meenakshi temple, where his statue on horseback stands at the entrance."
      },
      {
        "id": "de-nobili",
        "name": "Roberto de Nobili",
        "native": "தத்துவ போதக சுவாமி",
        "life": "1577 to 1656",
        "role": "Jesuit missionary",
        "steps": [
          4
        ],
        "text": "Born to a noble Roman family, de Nobili joined the Jesuits and sailed for India in 1604. At Madurai he adopted the dress, food and customs of a Brahmin ascetic, studied the Vedas and wrote in Tamil. Critics said he gave too much ground to caste; he defended himself and won. He spent his last years near Madras."
      },
      {
        "id": "tirumala",
        "name": "Tirumala Nayaka",
        "native": "திருமலை நாயக்கர்",
        "life": "died 1659",
        "role": "Nayaka of Madurai, 1623 to 1659",
        "steps": [
          6,
          7
        ],
        "text": "The best remembered of the Nayakas, Tirumala ruled for over thirty years, fought off Mysore and kept Madurai independent as Vijayanagara faded. He built his palace, the Pudhu Mandapam hall and the Teppakulam tank, and gave many gifts to temples. In his last years he admitted Bijapur and Golconda troops into the south, a move later held against him."
      },
      {
        "id": "mangammal",
        "name": "Rani Mangammal",
        "native": "ராணி மங்கம்மாள்",
        "life": "died about 1705",
        "role": "Regent of Madurai",
        "steps": [
          9
        ],
        "text": "Mangammal ruled Madurai as regent for about fifteen years, one of the few women to rule in the south. She was known for diplomacy, for her gifts to temples and also to Christian and Muslim causes, and for roads, tanks and rest houses. Accounts of her death differ; one, doubted by historians, says she was starved in prison."
      },
      {
        "id": "kattabomman",
        "name": "Veerapandiya Kattabomman",
        "native": "வீரபாண்டிய கட்டபொம்மன்",
        "life": "1760 to 1799",
        "role": "Poligar of Panchalankurichi",
        "steps": [
          11
        ],
        "text": "Kattabomman, chief of Panchalankurichi, refused to pay tribute to the East India Company. After a fight at the Company collector’s meeting at Ramanathapuram in 1798, his fort was attacked in 1799 and he fled. He was betrayed, tried before other poligars and hanged at Kayathar on 16 October 1799. He is a hero of Tamil song and film."
      }
    ]
  }
